 We begin by making a diagram. [asy]
import olympiad; import geometry; size(150); defaultpen(linewidth(0.8)); dotfactor=4;
real length = 2 * (6*sqrt(3) - 2), width = 6*sqrt(3);
draw(origin--(length,0)--(length,width)--(0,width)--cycle);
draw((length,width)--(0,2)^^(length,width)--(length - 6,0));
dot("$A$",origin,SW); dot("$B$",(length,0),SE); dot("$C$",(length,width),NE); dot("$D$",(0,width),NW); dot("$F$",(0,2),W); dot("$E$",(length - 6,0),S);
[/asy] <answer> The whole diagrams and triangles give $\angle B$ and $\angle C,$ so now we look at  $\triangle CEF.$

We know that angle $\angle C=180,$ and angle $\angle B$ is shared by both $\triangle CEF$ and $\triangle AED$, and we find that $\angle CEF= 60^{\circ}$.
Now we look at $\triangle EDC$. <See Brook Denver's solution.$\quad$ Look at@Looai's solution https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h2041802_the_triangle_problem.>

We find that the angle $\angle DCE = 45^{\circ}.$ This implies $\angle DFE= 45^{\circ}$ as well. We can now use the Law of Sines to compare how $CF$ compares to $CF.$ We find $$\frac{CD}{CF}=\sqrt{3},$$ $$\frac{CD}{CE}=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$$ and we know $CE=\sqrt{2}$ from the question, so therefore $$CD= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \times \sqrt{2}=1.\, \quad \text{ and } \,\quad AB= CF+ AE + BE = 5 + \sqrt{3}.$$
Therefore the area of the rectangle is $5\sqrt{3}+3\, units^{2}\,$

</answer>

